WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
- It's time for my first guest.

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.050
He's an Emmy winner for
his role on Will and Grace.

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.500
He's also the insanely
successful producer of shows like

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.680
Grimm, Hot in Cleveland
and Hollywood Game Night.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.250
Right now he's hosting a
podcast alongside his friends,

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.250
Jason Bateman, and Will
Arnett called Smartless.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.400
Sean Hayes, get in here.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.940
- Oh my God, wait, are we starting?

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:21.059
This is it?

00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:25.740
- (laughs loudly) I really
want you to intro the show

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:26.603
just like that.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:29.520
Oh my gosh, wait.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.770
- Wait, because I didn't,
I didn't speak to anybody,

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.763
but you just kind of
appeared like Jesus Christ--

00:00:33.763 --> 00:00:35.410
- I just, and that's how life is,

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:37.720
I just appear and just bless your life.

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.520
- It's incredible.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.933
- So wait, is it true that,
so you and Scott y'all added

00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:44.690
to your family during lockdown?

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:45.523
- Yes, we did.

00:00:45.523 --> 00:00:47.630
Do you wanna see this person?

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.978
- Yes, because I have a Goldendoodle.

00:00:49.978 --> 00:00:51.165
- Oh, you do?

00:00:51.165 --> 00:00:53.290
- Yes, Henry come here.
- Wait, I wanna see.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:54.400
- He was just in here a minute ago.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.590
- Is he awake?

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:58.434
(whistles)

00:00:58.434 --> 00:00:59.746
- Where you at love?

00:00:59.746 --> 00:01:02.200
Come here, come here,
come here, oh my God.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.780
- Oh my gosh he's huge.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:05.470
- He was supposed to be a miniature...

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:08.610
Hi, he was supposed to be
a miniature, but he's not.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.580
He's like 40 pounds (chuckles)

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.023
His name is Henry.

00:01:13.915 --> 00:01:16.013
(gasps) Stop it!

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:18.470
Oh my God.

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.620
- I woke him up and he's sleeping.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.060
- Does he have puppy breath?

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.172
- Yes.

00:01:23.172 --> 00:01:24.140
(Kelly gasps)

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.500
If puppy breath means horrible,
horrible breath like mine,

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:28.471
then yes.

00:01:28.471 --> 00:01:29.304
- Oh my God, in the morning, yeah.

00:01:29.304 --> 00:01:30.500
No, it's so funny.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.270
So how big is your Goldendoodle gonna get?

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:33.377
Is that a miniature or
normal, like a regular?

00:01:33.377 --> 00:01:35.590
- It's normal, it's normal.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:40.590
His parents are, you know,
like 50 to 60 pounds.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.110
- Well, this one was
supposed to be 15 to 18,

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.100
lies and deception.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.880
You're about 40.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:47.780
But he is the sweet...

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:48.640
Goldendoodles or so...

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.380
So the reason why I went
for it, is 'cause we had...

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:53.350
Oh my God, I used to hold him like a baby.

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.870
- He's so cute.

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.530
- Yeah, they're the best, right?

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:57.710
Especially in quarantine.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:00.330
'Cause it can be super depressing.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.590
- And I only have like, it's not that bad,

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:04.040
I only have like five nannies for him.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.990
Can you, can you come and
see, watch, I just do this.

00:02:07.990 --> 00:02:09.480
Can you just, thank you so much.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.003
- One, hello?

00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:16.460
One feeds, one walks, one naps with,

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:17.710
I love your five nannies.

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:19.640
- Wait, and then Scotty,

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.540
will you put a treat in this?

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:23.373
Okay, thanks.

00:02:23.373 --> 00:02:25.180
- Wait, did you tell me his name?

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:26.013
I didn't, I missed it.

00:02:26.013 --> 00:02:27.620
- Oh his name is Ricky.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:28.870
- Ricky, I love it.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:31.250
Like Ricky, Bobby, or what
did we name Ricky after?

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.750
- Scotty named him Ricky.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.112
I don't know why, why Scotty?

00:02:36.112 --> 00:02:36.945
- [Scotty] Just because--

00:02:36.945 --> 00:02:38.650
- Just 'cause, it's the first
name that came to his head.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:39.483
- [Kelly] I love it.

00:02:39.483 --> 00:02:42.910
So his new podcast is called
Smartless, which is awesome.

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:44.240
So tell me about the podcast.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.500
Why is it called Smartless?

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:48.670
- Yeah so Smartless, Jason actually...

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:50.890
Smartless has three hosts.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:53.300
It's me, Jason Bateman, and Will Arnett.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.262
And no, we don't talk
about Ozark the whole time.

00:02:55.262 --> 00:02:56.431
- (laughs loudly) I love--

00:02:56.431 --> 00:02:58.453
- It comes up a lot
though, It comes up a lot.

00:02:59.602 --> 00:03:04.270
But we've been friends for
20 something, 20-ish years

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:05.500
and known each other.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:09.090
Yeah, and we just played poker decades ago

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:10.930
every Tuesday night.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.244
And so we just thought, why don't we...

00:03:13.244 --> 00:03:14.230
We've always wanted to work together

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.700
and we've always all three
work together, like separately,

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:17.920
but never together.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:20.900
And so like let's get
into the podcast thing

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.970
and have an excuse to hang out every week

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:24.710
and talk about stuff we don't know about

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:26.040
because we're all idiots.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.550
And talk about people
who know more than us

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:29.720
and that's why it's called Smartless

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.680
because we wish we were smarter.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.530
And so it's famous people,
it's not famous people.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.300
It's people from all
different walks of life,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:38.780
like Neil deGrasse Tyson comes on

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.270
and talks about astrophysicist.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:41.103
I mean talks about--

00:03:41.103 --> 00:03:42.480
- I love him.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:43.580
- Yeah, he's the best.

00:03:45.259 --> 00:03:46.092
- I mean I love him

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:48.700
and then he can like
scare the crap out of you.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:50.117
- Yeah, like the worlds
gonna end tomorrow--

00:03:50.117 --> 00:03:52.100
- Like I didn't realize the
definition of black hole

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.260
would literally send me into
nightmares for like a month.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.090
- Yeah, and that the earth
is gonna get eaten up

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:57.490
by black hole one day, so thanks.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:03:59.560
- It's like, oh, solid.
- Merry Christmas!

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.670
- Yeah, that's awesome, I love that.

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:03.850
I love to hear that you're just...

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:06.460
That must be a cool
outlet especially now too.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:07.750
- We started, we were gonna do...

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:11.030
We started talking about it
and organizing it in January.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:13.540
So we were gonna do it anyway,
and then this happened.

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:15.450
So it just kind of worked out perfectly.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:16.980
- Yeah, that's awesome.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.810
Okay, so one thing I
love about your podcast,

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:20.871
y'all's podcast is that,
is it true that y'all,

00:04:20.871 --> 00:04:23.550
you bring someone and they
have no idea who's coming

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:24.607
and then they bring someone

00:04:24.607 --> 00:04:25.440
and then you have no idea

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:26.273
who you're gonna be interviewing?

00:04:26.273 --> 00:04:27.500
- Right, so every episode,

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.900
one of us acts as the
host and brings on a guest

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.367
that the other two don't know about.

00:04:31.367 --> 00:04:34.300
And so it's a surprise
reveal to the other two

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:35.430
and the audience.

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.840
Although marketing, we'll let
the audience know who's on,

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.860
but so it's cool in the
moment the other two

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:43.620
become kind of the
audience and ask questions

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:44.453
off the top of their head

00:04:44.453 --> 00:04:45.990
where the other one drives the interview.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:47.487
- Oh my gosh, I love that.

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:49.800
Has it ever happened to where
one of them has been like,

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.733
yay (laughs loudly) you know?

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:56.230
- You know what, for me, I
know nothing about sports.

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:57.306
So I'm like, I don't really know--

00:04:57.306 --> 00:04:58.139
- Me either.

00:04:58.139 --> 00:05:00.480
- And then I learn about the
person and I'm fascinated.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.540
So I love when I'm surprised
when the other two brings,

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:04.680
someone I don't know about.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.570
- I wanna be a surprise, can
I be your surprise one day?

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:08.403
Or do you not (indistinct)

00:05:08.403 --> 00:05:09.310
Does it matter?

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:10.710
- Yes, yes, yes.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:14.180
I would love that, that'd be
so fun, they would flip out.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.750
- Well, I kind of, you said
that y'all played poker.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:18.510
I kind of have trouble,

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.850
like thinking that you have
a really good poker face.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.073
'Cause I've played games with you.

00:05:22.073 --> 00:05:24.466
(laughing)

00:05:24.466 --> 00:05:25.767
like I don't have a
good poker face either.

00:05:25.767 --> 00:05:28.480
I'm literally like, I get
a good hand and I'm like.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:29.920
- Yeah, I start laughing.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:31.310
I'll just start laughing.

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:33.420
I know I'm the worst if
somebody's like, yeah.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:34.930
It's the worst.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:36.180
- I love poker though that's fun.

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:37.340
Y'all should do that again.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:38.400
That's fun.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:39.810
- Yeah, or virtually, yeah.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:40.920
- All right, all right.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.680
So, okay, well let me try and experiment.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:44.990
Try, I want you to try
and keep a straight face.

00:05:44.990 --> 00:05:46.650
'Cause I know that you're gonna laugh.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:49.260
While I give you something to
kind of spark your interest.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.150
So I'm gonna try out your
bluff face, are you ready?

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:52.030
- Okay, okay.

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:52.863
- [Kelly] You have to be serious.

00:05:52.863 --> 00:05:53.831
- I'm already losing it.

00:05:53.831 --> 00:05:55.440
I already lost, okay go.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.203
- Okay, cool, banana bread.

00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:01.210
You can't hold your face (laughs)

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:02.916
Okay, new puppy!

00:06:02.916 --> 00:06:03.749
(Sean laughs)

00:06:03.749 --> 00:06:04.850
Do you not---

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:05.683
Okay, cool.

00:06:05.683 --> 00:06:07.100
Now just don't smile.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:08.930
- Okay (laughs)

00:06:10.441 --> 00:06:11.296
That's really funny.

00:06:11.296 --> 00:06:13.670
- We're like the same person
in male and female form.

00:06:13.670 --> 00:06:14.620
I couldn't do it either.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:17.660
Okay, so I have to know about this.

00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:20.540
I heard, my producers are telling me,

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:23.443
did you steal a sandwich
from someone at some point

00:06:23.443 --> 00:06:25.870
and they you ended up
like literally in jail?

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:27.484
- Yeah, I sure did--

00:06:27.484 --> 00:06:30.630
- I gotta know this story
'cause that's an awesome story.

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:31.950
- Yeah, I think I was 18 years old,

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:33.550
well, I was 18 years old.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.870
I was in my freshman year in
college and I had no money

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.980
and just like zero money.

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.487
I don't know how I even bought a book--

00:06:42.487 --> 00:06:44.210
- That was me at age 18 as well, yes.

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:47.870
- Right, and so we we
all went out one night

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.690
and we were walking back from a party

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:53.460
and there was a kind
of like a, not 7-Eleven

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:55.150
but a 7-Eleven type store.

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:57.210
And so he walked in and
I was like, oh my God,

00:06:57.210 --> 00:06:58.830
I'm starving haven't eaten all day.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.550
And I stole a Turkey sandwich.

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:04.650
And so I walked outside with
my friends and I'm like,

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:05.650
eew it has tomatoes on it.

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:07.370
So we started throwing
tomatoes in the street

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:11.560
because we were dorks and
losers and then I'm eating it

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:14.510
and we're all laughing and comes up a cop,

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:15.940
stands right in front of me.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.570
And I just see like his badge
and I look up to his face.

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:20.150
He's like, you need to come with me.

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:21.530
And I'm like, are you joking me?

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:22.430
He's like, no.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:25.200
So I get into the cop
car, I had to go to jail.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:26.990
This kid bailed me out, his name is Liam.

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:27.823
He was the nicest guy...

00:07:27.823 --> 00:07:28.820
I didn't know him.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:29.960
And he gave me the a hundred bucks--

00:07:29.960 --> 00:07:31.720
- You didn't know him
and he bailed you out?

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:33.370
- Yeah because it was part of like,

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:35.010
you know, it was freshman year in college.

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:36.300
Everybody's kind of meeting everybody.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.980
So I didn't know him that well,
like I, but he was so nice.

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:42.430
And so I borrowed the a 100$
from my brother the next day.

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:45.740
And I paid him back and then cut to,

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:48.570
I don't know how many years
later, I'm in Los Angeles

00:07:48.570 --> 00:07:51.040
and I met at cafe and the guy comes up,

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:53.250
my waiter and he goes, do you remember me?

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:57.840
I was like, no, he goes, I'm
Liam, I bailed you out of jail.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:07:58.673
- No way!

00:07:58.673 --> 00:07:59.710
- I was like, wait, what?

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:00.543
That's so crazy.

00:08:00.543 --> 00:08:02.100
'Cause I hadn't seen
him after he did that.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.610
He liked transferred schools
or something or left.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:06.340
And, isn't that crazy?

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:07.173
- That is crazy,

00:08:07.173 --> 00:08:09.210
well, what's crazier is I
have a surprise for you,

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:10.760
Liam, come on in the Zoom room.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:12.897
No, I'm just kidding.

00:08:12.897 --> 00:08:14.640
(laughing loudly)

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:17.281
- Could you imagine, could you imagine?

00:08:17.281 --> 00:08:19.470
- I'm just kidding I just
wanted to see your bluff face

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:21.202
and by the way it looks
like this, oh (bleep)

00:08:21.202 --> 00:08:23.870
(laughing)

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:25.430
All right, I'm just kidding.

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:26.803
I was checking your poker
face, we don't have anything.

00:08:26.803 --> 00:08:28.520
- Yeah no, I have none, I have none.

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:30.100
But if you probably had him on,

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:32.390
that would be the best surprise ever.

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:34.150
I always wonder what happened to him.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:35.370
- I know I do love running into people.

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:38.570
I had so many odd jobs
before this or all these jobs

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:39.403
and I do...

00:08:39.403 --> 00:08:41.310
It's funny when you
randomly run into people

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:43.200
and it's out of context
and then you're like,

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.470
it's almost like certain smells bring back

00:08:45.470 --> 00:08:47.710
such nostalgic memories or certain things.

00:08:47.710 --> 00:08:48.543
It's the same thing.

00:08:48.543 --> 00:08:50.150
You're like, oh my God.,
it was a lifetime ago.

00:08:50.150 --> 00:08:54.440
- Well in some sense smell
is the number one sense

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:56.760
associated with memory.

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:58.324
- Yes, no, I didn't know that.

00:08:58.324 --> 00:08:59.157
I didn't know that.

00:08:59.157 --> 00:09:01.220
One thing we like to do
in these remote shows

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:02.780
is get a feel for our guests homes.

00:09:02.780 --> 00:09:05.030
Now we've already seen a
little bit of his media room,

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:07.910
I'm aware, but I thought
we could see a little more.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:09.810
So I love games, he loves games.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:12.650
I love winning, he might probably lose.

00:09:12.650 --> 00:09:14.008
Might probably that's good.

00:09:14.008 --> 00:09:17.730
So Sean has agreed to join
me in a friendly competition.

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:20.105
It's time to play Zoom Around The Room.

00:09:20.105 --> 00:09:22.740
(soft music)

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:23.910
All right, it's simple.

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:26.000
Parents, you can even play
this at home with your kids.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.050
I know that you're running
out of the circus thing

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:30.290
of trying to entertain your kids, me too.

00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:31.540
So here's how it's done.

00:09:31.540 --> 00:09:33.490
We'll be given a random word or phrase

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:35.360
by my executive producer, Alex.

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.990
Then we each run and find an
object to match that word.

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:42.160
And whoever gets back to their
camera first with the item

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:43.730
gets the point.

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:45.030
- Remember I just turned 50.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:46.240
Remember I just turned 50.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:48.970
- Well remember I might've
just moved out of this house

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:50.700
so I don't know what's here (laughs)

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:52.670
But I do have my running shoes on, so--

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:54.210
- Oh good!

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:55.530
- It looks like a cute dress,

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:57.354
but really I'm sporting
panting, I'm a working girl.

00:09:57.354 --> 00:09:58.187
- Me too.

00:09:58.187 --> 00:09:59.020
- Yeah, there you go.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:01.000
All right well, don't slip,
those might be slippery.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:02.723
Okay. Alex, go.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:05.196
- [Alex] Wooden.

00:10:05.196 --> 00:10:06.529
- [Kelly] Wooden!

00:10:06.529 --> 00:10:07.468
- Oh wait.

00:10:07.468 --> 00:10:08.519
(clock ticking)

00:10:08.519 --> 00:10:09.751
- Well, I got it, right here.

00:10:09.751 --> 00:10:11.589
(clock ticking)

00:10:11.589 --> 00:10:12.422
oh (bleep)

00:10:12.422 --> 00:10:14.601
(laughs loudly)

00:10:14.601 --> 00:10:15.700
- I was sitting in it,
I was sitting in it.

00:10:15.700 --> 00:10:17.080
- Oh you were sitting in a chair.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:18.140
You win, you win.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:19.620
I had a guitar, it was right behind.

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:22.710
I'm just happy I had
something wouldn't in here.

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:24.770
This is a guitar that I've never played.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:26.040
Actually, this one isn't mine.

00:10:26.040 --> 00:10:29.750
So yes, okay, I'm gonna
put this back up, hold on.

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:32.830
So it's still pretty
in the set, here we go.

00:10:32.830 --> 00:10:33.960
Oh God!

00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:35.830
- Look at your doggy, I love him.

00:10:35.830 --> 00:10:37.490
- Oh, Henry is the best.

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:40.520
He literally is the
greatest dog, I lucked out.

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:42.050
Okay, what's the next word?

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:43.310
Sean gets that one.

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:44.530
Adorable.

00:10:44.530 --> 00:10:46.190
- Adorable.

00:10:46.190 --> 00:10:47.490
- I'm just gonna sit here.

